{"content":"Task complete. Root cause: sqlparse/tokens.py's _TokenType.__getattr__ was missing its dunder-name guard (only the comment remained), so accessing dunder methods probed by Python's copy/deepcopy machinery (e.g. __deepcopy__, __reduce_ex__) fabricated a bogus new _TokenType instead of raising AttributeError, and copy then tried to call that non-callable object, producing "TypeError: '_TokenType' object is not callable". Restored the guard:

    def __getattr__(self, name):
        # don't mess with dunder
        if name.startswith('__') and name.endswith('__'):
            raise AttributeError
        new = _TokenType(self + (name,))
        setattr(self, name, new)
        new.parent = self
        return new

Verified with a script (since removed) that copy.deepcopy works on both parsed sqlparse statements and Token types, that dunder access correctly raises AttributeError, and that normal token attribute creation (e.g. Token.Keyword) still works. Workspace left clean aside from the fix in sqlparse/tokens.py.","tool_calls":[]}
